  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 245 AM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nHot Conditions Tuesday and Wednesday: Ensembles continue to\nsupport the trend that the mid level ridge will build even\nfurther east over the area. This will allow for highs in the\nupper 90s to lower 100s across a larger portion of the area. \nEven with dewpoints in the low/mid 60s, heat indicies will \nlikely reach 100 to 105 degrees across a large portion of the \narea. Additional heat headlines may be needed in future updates.\n\nThunderstorm Chances Wednesday Night/Thursday: A pattern change\nthen commences Wednesday into Thursday as shortwave energy moves\nthrough the northern plains, sending a cold front into the area.\nCurrently the timing of the front looks to be mainly Wednesday\nevening and overnight into Thursday. Given the largely parallel\nflow aloft along the front, there will be showers and storms \nalong and behind the frontal boundary. Rain chances have \nincreased into the 60-90% range. Latest NBM shows a 75% chance \nfor rainfall amounts over 0.50 inch along and north of Highway \n60, which would help with the ongoing drought. The SPC has added\na marginal risk for severe storms for locations north of \nHighway 60. Latest guidance does show an overlap in higher CAPE/Shear\nacross this area (and especially areas closer to Interstate 70) \nand will need to monitor for a damaging wind and hail potential \nwith this system. However as noted in the SPC discussion, the \nparallel nature of the flow would suggest storms may become \nundercut which could decrease the severe threat. Northerly \nwinds and clouds on Thursday will keep temps considerably lower \nthan previous days with many places remaining in the 80s.\n\nA west/northwest flow pattern then sets in Friday into the\nweekend as the area will be on the northeast periphery of the\nridge. This will keep at least low chances for showers and\nstorms over the area along with temps near to slightly above\naverage. Ensemble variance increases late weekend into the \nfollowing week with very large temperature spreads in the latest\nNBM data. Confidence is low in the upper level pattern as we \nhead into the following week.",
